WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.850
Improvisation is a great tool for actors and
directors.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.269
It improves morale on the set because it makes
the performers feel like they're a larger

00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:12.580
part of the creative process as they try to
find lines that are better or funnier than

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.580
the screenplay.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.830
But sometimes, a noble attempt at improv cracks
up the cast and leads to an unusable outtake.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.270
For example ...

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.510
Anchorman

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:25.120
A laughter-free take of this scene made it
into the credits of this classic retro comedy,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.090
including a line Will Ferrell apparently made
up on the spot, but that doesn't mean the

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:30.250
whole take was laughter-free.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:35.000
It comes early in the movie, when Ron Burgundy
is conducting vocal warm-ups in the minutes

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.100
leading up to live news broadcast.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.170
One is so ridiculous that even a pro like
Ferrell can't handle it ...

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.649
"The skeleton ran out of shampoo in the shower."

00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:46.200
"The Human Torch could not get a bank loan."

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.060
"The Hu— "

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.840
"The Human Torch was denied a bank loan."

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:56.780
Anchorman 2

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:01.720
While filming the sequel to 2004's Anchorman,
writer-director Adam McKay threw out line

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:07.220
suggestions on-the-spot for young actor Judah
Nelson — one involving warm apple juice.The

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.780
kid gives it his all, but he can't quite get
through it …

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.290
"Can you yell, 'I don't want to drink hot
frothy horse p---?'"

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.100
"I don't wanna drink hor ... forthy …"

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.330
This is 40

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.380
Judd Apatow is a director that likes to feed
improvised lines to his actors.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.700
While Billie Joe Armstrong of Green Day has
no problem playing punk rock in front of thousands

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.840
of rowdy fans, repeating one of Apatow's off-the-cuff
jokes while filming This is 40 made him lose

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.050
his composure.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.670
Maybe it hit a little too close to home?

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.580
"Yeah, I'm really trying to get back in touch
with my anger."

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.940
"It's hard to stay angry when you're this
rich."

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.140
"It's hard to stay angry when — "

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.940
The 40-Year-Old Virgin

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.060
Some actors and directors work together so
often that they develop a rapport that results

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.710
in movie magic — real-life couple Leslie
Mann and Judd Apatow are definitely on that

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:00.710
list.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.470
In this clip from the filming of The 40-Year-Old
Virgin, it's clear that Apatow treats Mann

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:09.170
like any other actor, providing on-the-spot
line suggestions to improve the scene.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.469
Mann is game at first, but then breaks character
when Apatow crosses the line ...

00:02:12.469 --> 00:02:14.090
"Do you wanna f--- in the car?"

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.180
"So, do you wanna f--- in the car?"

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.180
"No!"

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.360
"Do you like xtc?

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.120
Let's do some 'E' at my house."

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.120
"What?

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:20.680
Xtc?

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:21.680
Honey, we have kids!"

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.600
Trainwreck

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.560
LeBron James is obviously a great basketball
player, but who would've thought he'd be such

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.480
a charming and funny actor?

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.959
He holds his own as a thrifty, sensitive version
of himself in Trainwreck opposite formidable

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:36.870
comic talents like Amy Schumer and Bill Hader.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.739
James rolls with all the improvised lines
fed to him, cracking up his co-stars …

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:42.310
"I had tortilla soup.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:43.430
I can't get a hamburger.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.450
Look at how big I am.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.595
You think a tortilla soup is gonna last me
for the rest of the day?"

00:02:48.595 --> 00:02:49.879
The Hangover

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:54.560
When your movie stars a veteran of The Office
and The Daily Show and a one-of-a-kind standup

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.200
comedian, it's best to just let them do their
thing.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.829
In this scene from the first entry in the
Hangover trilogy, Ed Helms and Zach Galifianakis

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:07.060
engage in a rapidly escalating war of words,
reaching absurd, specific, and ridiculous

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.060
heights …

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.420
"You look like someone puked hair on your
chest."

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.340
"You look like someone puked hair on your
face."

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:16.180
"Guys, did you see his body?"

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.920
"You look like you just swallowed two frozen
turkeys.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:19.920
Happy Thanksgiving."

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:21.780
"What?"

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.659
Thanks for watching!

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:26.510
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.829
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

